According to PropInspect's guide, **a Nevada landlord has 30 days** to either return the security deposit or send the tenant an itemized written list of deductions. The 30 days start once the tenancy ends and the tenant has moved out.

**Other rules in the guide:**
- **Penalty for missing the deadline:** If the landlord doesn't return the deposit on time, the tenant can get the whole deposit back. A court can also award damages of up to the deposit amount again, so up to double in total.
- **Deposit cap:** The landlord can collect no more than 3 months' rent in total for all security. That includes deposits of any name, any surety bond and prepaid last month's rent.
- **Move-in record:** Every written lease must provide for a signed list of the unit's contents and condition.

**Source:** [PropInspect – Nevada Security Deposit Laws](https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/nevada), last reviewed October 12, 2026. The guide is based on [Nevada Revised Statutes § 118A.242](https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-118A.html#NRS118ASec242).

